so what time is it  it's early on easter sunday morning we  remember that  inferno started on good friday morning  and then proceeded through friday night  and holy saturday what rest there was  was the kind of rest you get in hell  maybe as the old saying goes a bad night  in a bad motel something like that  when dante and virgil emerge now at the  beginning of purgatorio  it is into this new day  and to this moment near dawn on easter  sunday  it's interesting to think by the way of  the inferno as a journey that takes  place  across holy saturday students of course  love to point out that dante's quest  resembles  one of their more uh frequent  and frequently lamented practices the  all-nighter  so when dante and virgil emerge um  it's after that journey and now finally  on the shores of purgatory  let's jump in to canto one and notice a  few things together  i will sing about that second realm  given the human soul  to purge its sin and grow worthy to  climb to paradise  hear rise to life again dead  poetry this beautiful  striking opening so probably the most  interesting line is that last one though  hear rise to life again dead poetry  and i wish someone had told me at least  a little bit earlier  that we were reading dead poetry but we  learn about it now  or so it seems it's not entirely clear  what that line means  at a minimum here on easter sunday  morning  poetry itself is going to rise to new  life  no more poetry of death no more poetry  of despair  no more poetry of the lost  while the poem is certainly about the  state of souls after death  and these three parts of eternity  it's also about the present moment it's  about the here and now  and so one of the great fruits of the  poem is to help  us the readers in the present moment of  our lives  in our present duties in the here and  now  one of the things dante does better than  anyone  is to help give the reader a  perspective on life that's  reliable and trustworthy and helpful  it's very easy to lose perspective in  life it's very easy to  have a skewed perspective and what dante  does is he gives us  an eternal perspective on the soul  on freedom on love on god and that's one  of the greatest gifts  any teacher could give to a student any  poet  to a reader any artist to an audience  the beginning of dante's purgatory
 

